## an6.70
an6.70:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, it is quite impossible that a mendicant without immersion that is peaceful, refined, tranquil, and unified will wield the many kinds of psychic power: multiplying themselves and becoming one again; materializing and dematerializing; going unobstructed through a wall, a rampart, or a mountain as if through space; diving in and out of the earth as if it were water; walking on water as if it were earth; flying cross-legged through the sky like a bird; touching and stroking with the hand the sun and moon, so mighty and powerful. They control the body as far as the realm of divinity. | T: “Monks, it’s impossible that a monk—with concentration that is not peaceful, not exquisite, not attained through calming, not immersed in unification—would wield manifold supranormal powers; that having been one he would become many; that having been many he would become one; that he would appear, vanish, go unimpeded through walls, ramparts, & mountains as if through space; that he would dive in & out of the earth as if it were water; that he would walk on water without sinking as if it were dry land; that sitting cross-legged he would fly through the air like a winged bird; that with his hand he would touch & stroke even the sun & moon, so mighty & powerful; that he would influence with his body even as far as the Brahmā worlds.
an6.70:1.2 | S: It’s impossible that with clairaudience that is purified and superhuman, they’ll hear both kinds of sounds, human and heavenly, whether near or far. | T: “It’s impossible that he would hear—by means of the divine ear-element, purified & surpassing the human—both kinds of sounds: divine & human, whether near or far.
an6.70:1.3 | S: It’s impossible that they’ll understand the minds of other beings and individuals, having encompassed them with their own mind, | T: “It’s impossible that he would discern the awareness of other beings, other individuals, having encompassed it with his own awareness;
an6.70:1.4 | S: understanding mind with greed as ‘mind with greed’ … | T: that he would discern a mind with passion as ‘a mind with passion,’ and a mind without passion as ‘a mind without passion’; that he would discern a mind with aversion as ‘a mind with aversion,’ and a mind without aversion as ‘a mind without aversion’; that he would discern a mind with delusion as ‘a mind with delusion,’ and a mind without delusion as ‘a mind without delusion’; that he would discern a restricted mind as ‘a restricted mind,’ and a scattered mind as ‘a scattered mind’; that he would discern an enlarged mind as ‘an enlarged mind,’ and an unenlarged mind as ‘an unenlarged mind’; that he would discern a surpassed mind [one that is not at the most excellent level] as ‘a surpassed mind,’ and an unsurpassed mind as ‘an unsurpassed mind’; that he would discern a concentrated mind as ‘a concentrated mind,’ and an unconcentrated mind as ‘an unconcentrated mind’;
an6.70:1.5 | S: and freed mind as ‘freed mind’. | T: that he would discern a released mind as ‘a released mind,’ and an unreleased mind as ‘an unreleased mind.’
an6.70:1.6 | S: It’s impossible that they’ll recollect many kinds of past lives, with features and details. | T: “It’s impossible that he would recollect his manifold past lives [literally: previous homes], i.e., one birth, two births, three births, four, five, ten, twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, one hundred, one thousand, one hundred thousand, many eons of cosmic contraction, many eons of cosmic expansion, many eons of cosmic contraction & expansion, (recollecting,) ‘There I had such a name, belonged to such a clan, had such an appearance. Such was my food, such my experience of pleasure & pain, such the end of my life. Passing away from that state, I re-arose there. There too I had such a name, belonged to such a clan, had such an appearance. Such was my food, such my experience of pleasure & pain, such the end of my life. Passing away from that state, I re-arose here’; that he would thus recollect his manifold past lives in their modes & details.
an6.70:1.7 | S: It’s impossible that with clairvoyance that is purified and surpasses the human, they’ll understand how sentient beings pass on according to their deeds. | T: “It’s impossible that he would see—by means of the divine eye, purified & surpassing the human—beings passing away & re-appearing, and discern how they are inferior & superior, beautiful & ugly, fortunate & unfortunate in accordance with their kamma: ‘These beings—who were endowed with bad conduct of body, speech, & mind, who reviled the noble ones, held wrong views and undertook actions under the influence of wrong views—with the break-up of the body, after death, have re-appeared in a plane of deprivation, a bad destination, a lower realm, hell. But these beings—who were endowed with good conduct of body, speech, & mind, who did not revile the noble ones, who held right views and undertook actions under the influence of right views—with the break-up of the body, after death, have re-appeared in a good destination, a heavenly world’; that he would thus—by means of the divine eye, purified & surpassing the human—see beings passing away & re-appearing, and discern how they are inferior & superior, beautiful & ugly, fortunate & unfortunate in accordance with their kamma.
an6.70:1.8 | S: It’s impossible that they’ll realize the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom, and live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: “It’s impossible that he would, through the ending of effluents, enter & remain in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, having directly known & realized them for himself right in the here & now.”
an6.70:2.1 | S: But it’s quite possible that a mendicant who has immersion that is peaceful, refined, tranquil, and unified will wield the many kinds of psychic power … | T: (none)
an6.70:2.2 | S: It’s possible that with clairaudience that is purified and superhuman, they’ll hear both kinds of sounds … | T: (none)
an6.70:2.3 | S: It’s possible that they’ll understand the minds of other beings … | T: (none)
an6.70:2.4 | P: sarāgaṁ vā cittaṁ sarāgaṁ cittanti pajānissati …pe… | T: (none)
an6.70:2.5 | P: vimuttaṁ vā cittaṁ vimuttaṁ cittanti pajānissatī’ti ṭhānametaṁ vijjati. | T: (none)
an6.70:2.6 | S: It’s possible that they’ll recollect many kinds of past lives, with features and details. | T: (none)
an6.70:2.7 | S: It’s possible that with clairvoyance that is purified and superhuman, they’ll understand how sentient beings pass on according to their deeds. | T: (none)
an6.70:2.8 | S: It’s possible that they’ll realize the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom, and live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements.” | T: (none)
an6.70:2.9 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.34
an9.34:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Sāriputta was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: I have heard that on one occasion Ven. Sāriputta was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Forest, the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
an9.34:1.2 | S: There he addressed the mendicants: | T: There he said to the monks,
an9.34:1.3 | S: “Reverends, extinguishment is bliss! | T: “This unbinding is pleasant, friends.
an9.34:1.4 | S: Extinguishment is bliss!” | T: This unbinding is pleasant.”
an9.34:1.5 | S: When he said this, Venerable Udāyī said to him, | T: When this was said, Ven. Udāyin said to Ven. Sāriputta,
an9.34:1.6 | S: “But Reverend Sāriputta, what’s blissful about it, since nothing is felt?” | T: “But what is the pleasure here, my friend, where there is nothing felt?”
an9.34:1.7 | S: “The fact that nothing is felt is precisely what’s blissful about it. | T: “Just that is the pleasure here, my friend: where there is nothing felt.
an9.34:1.8 | S: Reverend, there are these five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: There are these five strands of sensuality.
an9.34:1.9 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an9.34:1.10 | S: Sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: Forms cognizable via the eye—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked to sensual desire;
an9.34:1.11 | S: Sounds known by the ear … | T: sounds cognizable via the ear…
an9.34:1.12 | S: Smells known by the nose … | T: aromas cognizable via the nose…
an9.34:1.13 | S: Tastes known by the tongue … | T: flavors cognizable via the tongue…
an9.34:1.14 | S: Touches known by the body, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: tactile sensations cognizable via the body—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked to sensual desire.
an9.34:1.15 | S: These are the five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: (none)
an9.34:1.16 | S: The pleasure and happiness that arise from these five kinds of sensual stimulation is called sensual pleasure. | T: Whatever pleasure or joy arises in dependence on these five strands of sensuality, that is sensual pleasure.
an9.34:2.1 | S: First, take a mendicant who, quite secluded from sensual pleasures … enters and remains in the first absorption. | T: “Now there is the case where a monk—quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities—enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
an9.34:2.2 | S: While a mendicant is practicing such a meditation, if perception and focus accompanied by sensual pleasures beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: If, as he remains there, he is beset with attention to perceptions dealing with sensuality, that is an affliction for him.
an9.34:2.3 | S: Suppose a happy person were to experience pain; that would be an affliction for them. | T: Just as pain would arise in a healthy person as an affliction,
an9.34:2.4 | S: In the same way, should perception and focus accompanied by sensual pleasures beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: even so the attention to perceptions dealing with sensuality that beset the monk is an affliction for him.
an9.34:2.5 | S: And affliction has been called suffering by the Buddha. | T: Now, the Blessed One has said that whatever is an affliction is stress.
an9.34:2.6 | S: That’s the way to understand how extinguishment is bliss. | T: So by this line of reasoning it may be known how unbinding is pleasant.
an9.34:3.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, as the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, enters and remains in the second absorption. | T: “Then there is the case where a monk, with the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, enters & remains in the second jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance.
an9.34:3.2 | S: While a mendicant is practicing such a meditation, if perception and focus accompanied by placing of the mind beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: If, as he remains there, he is beset with attention to perceptions dealing with directed thought, that is an affliction for him.…
an9.34:3.3 | S: Suppose a happy person were to experience pain; that would be an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:3.4 | S: In the same way, should perception and focus accompanied by placing of the mind beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:3.5 | S: And affliction has been called suffering by the Buddha. | T: (none)
an9.34:3.6 | S: That too is a way to understand how extinguishment is bliss. | T: (none)
an9.34:4.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, with the fading away of rapture, enters and remains in the third absorption. | T: “Then there is the case where a monk, with the fading of rapture, remains equanimous, mindful, & alert, senses pleasure with the body, and enters & remains in the third jhāna, of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous & mindful, he has a pleasant abiding.’
an9.34:4.2 | S: While a mendicant is practicing such a meditation, if perception and focus accompanied by rapture beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: If, as he remains there, he is beset with attention to perceptions dealing with rapture, that is an affliction for him.…
an9.34:4.3 | S: Suppose a happy person were to experience pain; that would be an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:4.4 | S: In the same way, should perception and focus accompanied by rapture beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:4.5 | S: And affliction has been called suffering by the Buddha. | T: (none)
an9.34:4.6 | S: That too is a way to understand how extinguishment is bliss. | T: (none)
an9.34:5.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, with the giving up of pleasure and pain and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, enters and remains in the fourth absorption. | T: “Then there is the case where a monk, with the abandoning of pleasure & pain—as with the earlier disappearance of elation & distress—enters & remains in the fourth jhāna: purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither pleasure nor pain.
an9.34:5.2 | S: While a mendicant is practicing such a meditation, if perception and focus accompanied by bliss with equanimity beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: If, as he remains there, he is beset with attention to perceptions dealing with equanimity, that is an affliction for him.…
an9.34:5.3 | S: Suppose a happy person were to experience pain; that would be an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:5.4 | S: In the same way, should perception and focus accompanied by bliss with equanimity beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:5.5 | S: And affliction has been called suffering by the Buddha. | T: (none)
an9.34:5.6 | S: That too is a way to understand how extinguishment is bliss. | T: (none)
an9.34:6.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, going totally beyond perceptions of form, with the disappearance of perceptions of impingement, not focusing on perceptions of diversity, aware that ‘space is infinite’, enters and remains in the dimension of infinite space. | T: “Then there is the case where a monk, with the complete transcending of perceptions of (physical) form, with the disappearance of perceptions of resistance, and not attending to perceptions of multiplicity, (perceiving,) ‘Infinite space,’ enters & remains in the dimension of the infinitude of space.
an9.34:6.2 | S: While a mendicant is practicing such a meditation, if perception and focus accompanied by form beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: If, as he remains there, he is beset with attention to perceptions dealing with form, that is an affliction for him.…
an9.34:6.3 | S: Suppose a happy person were to experience pain; that would be an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:6.4 | S: In the same way, should perception and focus accompanied by form beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:6.5 | S: And affliction has been called suffering by the Buddha. | T: (none)
an9.34:6.6 | S: That too is a way to understand how extinguishment is bliss. | T: (none)
an9.34:7.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, going totally beyond the dimension of infinite space, aware that ‘consciousness is infinite’, enters and remains in the dimension of infinite consciousness. | T: “Then there is the case where a monk, with the complete transcending of the dimension of the infinitude of space, (perceiving,) ‘Infinite consciousness,’ enters & remains in the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness.
an9.34:7.2 | S: While a mendicant is practicing such a meditation, if perception and focus accompanied by the dimension of infinite space beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: If, as he remains there, he is beset with attention to perceptions dealing with the dimension of the infinitude of space, that is an affliction for him.…
an9.34:7.3 | S: Suppose a happy person were to experience pain; that would be an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:7.4 | S: In the same way, should perception and focus accompanied by the dimension of infinite space beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:7.5 | S: And affliction has been called suffering by the Buddha. | T: (none)
an9.34:7.6 | S: That too is a way to understand how extinguishment is bliss. | T: (none)
an9.34:8.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, going totally beyond the dimension of infinite consciousness, aware that ‘there is nothing at all’, enters and remains in the dimension of nothingness. | T: “Then there is the case where a monk, with the complete transcending of the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness, (perceiving,) ‘There is nothing,’ enters & remains in the dimension of nothingness.
an9.34:8.2 | S: While a mendicant is practicing such a meditation, if perception and focus accompanied by the dimension of infinite consciousness beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: If, as he remains there, he is beset with attention to perceptions dealing with the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness, that is an affliction for him.…
an9.34:8.3 | S: Suppose a happy person were to experience pain; that would be an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:8.4 | S: In the same way, should perception and focus accompanied by the dimension of infinite consciousness beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:8.5 | S: And affliction has been called suffering by the Buddha. | T: (none)
an9.34:8.6 | S: That too is a way to understand how extinguishment is bliss. | T: (none)
an9.34:9.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, going totally beyond the dimension of nothingness, enters and remains in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. | T: “Then there is the case where a monk, with the complete transcending of the dimension of nothingness, enters & remains in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception.
an9.34:9.2 | S: While a mendicant is practicing such a meditation, if perception and focus accompanied by the dimension of nothingness beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: If, as he remains there, he is beset with attention to perceptions dealing with the dimension of nothingness, that is an affliction for him.
an9.34:9.3 | S: Suppose a happy person were to experience pain; that would be an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:9.4 | S: In the same way, should perception and focus accompanied by the dimension of nothingness beset them, that’s an affliction for them. | T: (none)
an9.34:9.5 | S: And affliction has been called suffering by the Buddha. | T: Now, the Blessed One has said that whatever is an affliction is stress.
an9.34:9.6 | S: That too is a way to understand how extinguishment is bliss. | T: So by this line of reasoning it may be known how unbinding is pleasant.
an9.34:10.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, going totally beyond the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, enters and remains in the cessation of perception and feeling. And, having seen with wisdom, their defilements come to an end. | T: “Then there is the case where a monk, with the complete transcending of the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, enters & remains in the cessation of perception & feeling. And as he sees (that) with discernment, effluents are completely ended.
an9.34:11.1 | S: That too is a way to understand how extinguishment is bliss.” | T: So by this line of reasoning it may be known how unbinding is pleasant.”
an9.34:11.2 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)


